// Physics Regents — June 2016
export default {
  id: 'phys-jun-2016',
  subject: 'physics',
  year: 2016,
  session: 'June',
  totalMinutes: 180,
  questions: [
    { number: 1, part: 'A', text: 'Which quantity is a vector?', choices: ['power', 'speed', 'kinetic energy', 'weight'], topic: 'Forces & Newton', correct: 3, explanation: 'Weight is a force (gravitational pull on a mass), and forces are vectors — they have both magnitude and direction. Power, speed, and kinetic energy are scalar quantities with magnitude only.', diveDeep: 'Vectors have both magnitude AND direction; scalars have magnitude only. Force vectors include weight, normal force, friction, tension, and applied force. Weight always points downward (toward Earth\'s center). Speed is the scalar counterpart to velocity (which is a vector). Kinetic energy and power are scalars — they have no direction. On the Regents, you must classify quantities as vectors (force, velocity, acceleration, displacement, momentum) or scalars (mass, time, temperature, speed, energy, power, work). A common trap: students confuse speed (scalar) with velocity (vector).' },
    { number: 2, part: 'A', text: 'A 65.0-kilogram astronaut weighs 638 newtons at the surface of Earth. What is the mass of the astronaut at the surface of the Moon, where the acceleration due to gravity is 1.62 meters per second squared?', choices: ['10.7 kg', '105 N', '65.0 kg', '638 N'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 2, explanation: 'Mass is a measure of the amount of matter in an object and does not change with location. The astronaut\'s mass remains 65.0 kg on the Moon, on Earth, or anywhere in the universe. Only weight (the gravitational force on the mass) changes with location.', diveDeep: 'Mass (kg) vs. weight (N): mass is invariant (same everywhere); weight = mg depends on local g. On Earth g = 9.8 m/s², so weight = 65.0 × 9.8 = 637 N ≈ 638 N. On the Moon g = 1.62 m/s², so weight = 65.0 × 1.62 = 105.3 N (choice B, which is a force in Newtons — note the choices include 105 N, which is the weight on the Moon, not the mass). Students often confuse choices B (105 N, weight on Moon) and C (65.0 kg, mass). The question asks for mass — always measured in kg, never Newtons.' },
    { number: 3, part: 'A', text: 'When the sum of all the forces acting on a block on an inclined plane is zero, the block', choices: ['must be at rest', 'must be accelerating', 'may be slowing down', 'may be moving at constant speed'], topic: 'Forces & Newton', correct: 3, image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q3.png', explanation: 'Newton\'s First Law: when the net force on an object is zero, the object is in equilibrium — it maintains its current state of motion. This means it could be at rest OR moving at constant velocity (constant speed in a straight line).', diveDeep: 'Zero net force = zero acceleration (Newton\'s 2nd Law: F_net = ma; if F_net = 0, then a = 0). Zero acceleration means constant velocity — which includes both "at rest" (v = 0, constant) and "moving at constant speed" (v ≠ 0, constant). Choice A says "must be at rest" — this is too restrictive; constant speed is also possible. Choice B (accelerating) requires a net force. Choice C (slowing down) requires a net force opposing motion. The key word in choice D is "may" — it is possible for the block to move at constant speed. On inclined planes, equilibrium means all component forces balance: normal force, friction, and the gravitational component parallel to the slope all cancel.' },
    { number: 4, part: 'A', text: 'The greatest increase in the inertia of an object would be produced by increasing the', choices: ['mass of the object from 1.0 kg to 2.0 kg', 'net force applied to the object from 1.0 N to 2.0 N', 'time that a net force is applied to the object from 1.0 s to 2.0 s', 'speed of the object from 1.0 m/s to 2.0 m/s'], topic: 'Forces & Newton', correct: 0, image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q4.png', explanation: 'Inertia is the resistance of an object to changes in its state of motion, and it is directly proportional to mass. Only increasing mass increases inertia. Net force, time, and speed have no effect on an object\'s inertia.', diveDeep: 'Inertia is not a force — it is the property of matter that resists acceleration. Mathematically, inertia = mass. Doubling mass from 1.0 to 2.0 kg doubles inertia. Force (choice B), time (choice C), and speed (choice D) do not affect an object\'s inertia. Common confusion: students think a faster object is harder to stop because it has "more inertia" — it\'s actually harder to stop because it has more momentum (p = mv), not more inertia. Heavy objects (large mass) have more inertia regardless of their speed. This is why a bowling ball is harder to start moving than a tennis ball — even at rest.' },
    { number: 5, part: 'A', text: 'A 100.-kilogram cart accelerates at 0.50 meter per second squared west as a horse exerts a force of 60. newtons west on the cart. What is the magnitude of the force that the cart exerts on the horse?', choices: ['10. N', '60. N', '50. N', '110 N'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 2, explanation: 'Wait — let\'s apply Newton\'s Third Law: the cart exerts an equal and opposite force on the horse, which equals 60 N east. But the question asks about forces via Newton\'s 2nd Law: F_net = ma = 100 × 0.50 = 50 N. The net force on the cart is 50 N west. The horse applies 60 N west, so friction/other forces must provide 10 N east. Newton\'s 3rd Law: the cart exerts 60 N on the horse. However, the correct answer listed is 60 N.', diveDeep: 'Newton\'s Third Law: for every action force, there is an equal and opposite reaction force. If the horse exerts 60 N west on the cart, the cart exerts 60 N east on the horse — always the same magnitude. Note: the net force on the cart is 50 N (from F = ma = 100 × 0.50), which means additional forces (friction) are also acting. But the specific force the cart exerts on the horse (Newton\'s 3rd Law pair) is 60 N. Students should identify action-reaction pairs: horse pushes cart (60 N west) → cart pushes horse (60 N east). These pairs are always equal in magnitude, opposite in direction, and act on DIFFERENT objects.' },
    { number: 6, part: 'A', text: 'Sound waves are described as', choices: ['mechanical and transverse', 'mechanical and longitudinal', 'electromagnetic and transverse', 'electromagnetic and longitudinal'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 1, explanation: 'Sound is a mechanical wave (requires a medium — cannot travel through vacuum) and longitudinal (particle vibrations are parallel to the direction of wave propagation, creating compressions and rarefactions).', diveDeep: 'Wave classification: Mechanical waves require a medium (sound, water waves, seismic waves). Electromagnetic waves require NO medium (light, radio, X-rays, microwaves). Transverse waves: particle displacement perpendicular to wave travel (EM waves, light, transverse seismic S-waves). Longitudinal waves: particle displacement parallel to wave travel — creates compressions (high density) and rarefactions (low density). Sound travels through compressions and rarefactions of air molecules. On the Regents: sound = mechanical + longitudinal; light = electromagnetic + transverse. These are frequently tested distinctions.' },
    { number: 7, part: 'A', text: 'An electrical force of 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ newton exists between two point charges, q₁ and q₂. If the distance between the charges is doubled, the new electrical force between the charges will be', choices: ['1.6 × 10⁻⁴ N', '3.2 × 10⁻⁴ N', '2.0 × 10⁻⁵ N', '4.0 × 10⁻⁵ N'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 2, explanation: 'Coulomb\'s Law: F = kq₁q₂/r². If distance r is doubled (r → 2r), force changes as F\' = kq₁q₂/(2r)² = kq₁q₂/4r² = F/4. Original force = 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ N; new force = 8.0 × 10⁻⁵ / 4 = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ N.', diveDeep: 'Coulomb\'s Law follows an inverse-square relationship with distance: F ∝ 1/r². This means: double distance → force decreases by factor of 4 (÷4); triple distance → force decreases by factor of 9 (÷9); halve distance → force increases by factor of 4 (×4). This is the same inverse-square relationship as Newton\'s Law of Gravitation. Choice A (1.6 × 10⁻⁴) is doubling — wrong direction. Choice B (3.2 × 10⁻⁴) is quadrupling — wrong. Choice D (4.0 × 10⁻⁵) is halving — incorrect calculation. The pattern to memorize: distance doubled → force ÷ 4 (inverse square law).' },
    { number: 8, part: 'A', text: 'A blue lab cart is traveling west on a track when it collides with and sticks to a red lab cart traveling east. The magnitude of the momentum of the blue cart before the collision is 2.0 kilogram·meters per second, and the magnitude of the momentum of the red cart before the collision is 3.0 kilogram·meters per second. The magnitude of the total momentum of the two carts after the collision is', choices: ['1.0 kg·m/s', '3.0 kg·m/s', '2.0 kg·m/s', '5.0 kg·m/s'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 0, image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q8.png', explanation: 'Momentum is a vector — direction matters. Blue cart: 2.0 kg·m/s west (negative); red cart: 3.0 kg·m/s east (positive). Total momentum = +3.0 − 2.0 = +1.0 kg·m/s east. The magnitude is 1.0 kg·m/s.', diveDeep: 'Conservation of momentum: total momentum before = total momentum after (closed system). Assign directions: east = positive. Blue (west) = −2.0 kg·m/s; Red (east) = +3.0 kg·m/s. Total = −2.0 + 3.0 = +1.0 kg·m/s (east). After the perfectly inelastic collision (they stick together), the combined carts move east with 1.0 kg·m/s of momentum. Choice D (5.0) adds magnitudes without considering direction — this is a common error when forgetting that momentum is a vector. Always assign signs to indicate direction before summing momenta. Kinetic energy is NOT conserved in perfectly inelastic collisions (they stick together), but momentum always is.' },
    { number: 9, part: 'A', text: 'The diagram below represents the path of a thrown ball through the air. Which arrow best represents the direction in which friction acts on the ball at point P?', choices: ['', '', '', ''], topic: 'Forces & Newton', correct: 1, image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q9.png', explanation: 'Air friction (drag) acts opposite to the velocity of the ball. At point P on the projectile\'s path, the ball is moving in a specific direction, so friction acts in the exact opposite direction to oppose that motion.', diveDeep: 'Air resistance (friction) always opposes the direction of motion — it acts antiparallel to velocity. For a projectile, the velocity vector changes direction throughout the path (starts at an upward angle, then curves downward). At any point P, friction is exactly opposite to the velocity at that point. Note: gravity always acts straight down, while friction opposes the velocity direction (which includes both horizontal and vertical components). Students sometimes assume friction acts horizontally only — it acts opposite to the net velocity vector, which has both horizontal and vertical components during projectile motion.' },
    { number: 10, part: 'A', text: 'A magnetic field would be produced by a beam of', choices: ['x rays', 'protons', 'gamma rays', 'neutrons'], topic: 'Magnetism', correct: 1, explanation: 'Moving electric charges create magnetic fields. Protons are positively charged particles, so a beam of moving protons constitutes a current and produces a magnetic field. X-rays, gamma rays, and neutrons are electrically neutral and do not produce magnetic fields by their motion.', diveDeep: 'Magnetic fields are produced by moving electric charges (Ampere\'s law). A beam of protons (charge +e each) moving in a direction constitutes a conventional current → produces a magnetic field (right-hand rule: point thumb in current direction, fingers curl in direction of B field). X-rays (choice A) are high-energy electromagnetic radiation — no charge. Gamma rays (choice C) are also EM radiation — no charge. Neutrons (choice D) have no electric charge. Even electrons (negative charge) moving in a beam would produce a magnetic field, but the resulting B field direction would be opposite to that from protons. Charged particle beams in particle accelerators produce significant magnetic fields.' },
    { number: 11, part: 'A', text: 'The diagram below represents the electric field in the region of two small charged spheres, A and B. What is the sign of the net charge on A and B?', choices: ['A is positive and B is positive.', 'A is positive and B is negative.', 'A is negative and B is negative.', 'A is negative and B is positive.'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 2, image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q11.png', explanation: 'Electric field lines point away from positive charges and toward negative charges. If field lines in the diagram point toward both A and B, both spheres are negatively charged. If lines point away from both, both are positive. The diagram shows lines pointing toward both spheres.', diveDeep: 'Electric field line rules: (1) lines originate at positive charges and terminate at negative charges; (2) field lines indicate the direction a positive test charge would move; (3) field strength is proportional to line density. To determine charge sign: if all field lines near a sphere point TOWARD it → negative charge; if lines point AWAY → positive charge. Between two negative charges, field lines point toward both (forming a pattern of lines entering both spheres). Between a positive and negative charge, field lines go from + to −. Students should practice reading field line diagrams and identifying charge signs from arrow directions.' },
    { number: 12, part: 'A', text: 'A horizontal force of 20 newtons eastward causes a 10-kilogram box to have a displacement of 5 meters eastward. The total work done on the box by the 20-newton force is', choices: ['40 J', '100 J', '50 J', '1000 J'], topic: 'Energy & Work', correct: 1, explanation: 'Work = Force × displacement × cos(θ). The force and displacement are both eastward, so θ = 0°, cos(0°) = 1. W = 20 N × 5 m × 1 = 100 J.', diveDeep: 'Work formula: W = Fd·cosθ, where θ is the angle between force and displacement vectors. When force and displacement are parallel (same direction): θ = 0°, cos(0°) = 1, W = Fd (maximum positive work). When force is perpendicular to displacement: θ = 90°, cos(90°) = 0, W = 0 (no work done — e.g., normal force on a horizontally moving object). When force opposes motion: θ = 180°, cos(180°) = −1, W = −Fd (negative work, object decelerates). Here: W = 20 × 5 = 100 J. Units: 1 J = 1 N·m. Students should not confuse force (N) with work (J).' },
    { number: 13, part: 'A', text: 'A block initially at rest on a horizontal, frictionless surface is accelerated by a constant horizontal force of 5.0 newtons. If 15 joules of work is done on the block by this force while accelerating it, the kinetic energy of the block increases by', choices: ['3.0 J', '15 J', '75 J', '50 J'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 1, explanation: 'The Work-Energy Theorem states that the net work done on an object equals its change in kinetic energy. Since the surface is frictionless, all 15 J of work goes directly into increasing the block\'s kinetic energy. ΔKE = W_net = 15 J.', diveDeep: 'Work-Energy Theorem: W_net = ΔKE = KE_final − KE_initial. On a frictionless surface, the only horizontal force is the 5.0 N applied force, so W_net = 15 J = ΔKE. If there were friction, some work would be converted to thermal energy, and ΔKE < W_applied. Starting from rest: KE_initial = 0, so KE_final = 15 J. We can also verify: W = Fd, 15 = 5.0 × d, so d = 3.0 m. Using kinematics: F = ma, KE = ½mv². All methods give ΔKE = 15 J. This is a fundamental connection between work and kinetic energy.' },
    { number: 14, part: 'A', text: 'Two objects, A and B, are held one meter above the horizontal ground. The mass of B is twice as great as the mass of A. If PE is the gravitational potential energy of A relative to the ground, then the gravitational potential energy of B relative to the ground is', choices: ['PE/2', 'PE', '2PE', '4PE'], topic: 'Forces & Newton', correct: 2, explanation: 'Gravitational potential energy: PE = mgh. Both objects are at the same height (h = 1 m). Since mass of B = 2× mass of A, PE_B = (2m_A)gh = 2(m_A·g·h) = 2PE_A = 2PE.', diveDeep: 'PE_grav = mgh. Key variables: m (mass), g (gravitational field strength = 9.8 N/kg), h (height above reference point). PE is directly proportional to mass: double mass → double PE (linear relationship). PE is also directly proportional to height: double height → double PE. Contrast with KE = ½mv², where KE is proportional to the square of velocity. If both objects were dropped from 1 m, they would reach the ground with the same speed (independent of mass) but object B would have twice the kinetic energy at the bottom. The conversion is PE → KE as objects fall.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q14.png' },
    { number: 15, part: 'A', text: 'What is the kinetic energy of a 55-kilogram skier traveling at 9.0 meters per second?', choices: ['2.5 × 10² J', '2.2 × 10³ J', '5.0 × 10² J', '4.9 × 10³ J'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 1, explanation: 'KE = ½mv² = ½ × 55 × (9.0)² = ½ × 55 × 81 = 27.5 × 81 = 2227.5 ≈ 2.2 × 10³ J.', diveDeep: 'KE = ½mv². Be careful: the velocity is SQUARED. Steps: (1) square the velocity: 9.0² = 81 m²/s²; (2) multiply by mass: 55 × 81 = 4455; (3) multiply by ½: 4455/2 = 2227.5 J ≈ 2.2 × 10³ J. Common errors: forgetting the ½ (gives 4455 ≈ 4.5 × 10³ — not among choices); not squaring velocity (gives ½ × 55 × 9 = 247.5 ≈ 2.5 × 10² — choice A, a trap). KE units: kg × (m/s)² = kg·m²/s² = J (joules). KE is always positive (scalar). On Regents exams, always verify units and avoid forgetting the square or the ½.' },
    { number: 16, part: 'A', text: 'A 5.09 × 10¹⁴-hertz electromagnetic wave is traveling through a transparent medium. The main factor that determines the speed of this wave is the', choices: ['nature of the medium', 'amplitude of the wave', 'phase of the wave', 'distance traveled through the medium'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 0, image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q16.png', explanation: 'The speed of a wave in a medium depends on the properties of that medium (density, elasticity for mechanical waves; permittivity and permeability for electromagnetic waves). For EM waves, the refractive index of the medium determines the speed. In vacuum, all EM waves travel at c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.', diveDeep: 'Speed of electromagnetic waves: in vacuum, all EM waves travel at c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s regardless of frequency. When EM waves enter a medium, their speed decreases: v = c/n, where n is the refractive index. Different media have different refractive indices (water n ≈ 1.33, glass n ≈ 1.5, air n ≈ 1.0003). Amplitude, phase, and distance traveled do not affect wave speed — they affect energy, timing, and displacement. The frequency (5.09 × 10¹⁴ Hz corresponds to visible light) also doesn\'t change speed; frequency is fixed by the source. Wavelength changes (λ = v/f) when entering a new medium, but frequency remains constant.' },
    { number: 17, part: 'A', text: 'A motor does a total of 480 joules of work in 5.0 seconds to lift a 12-kilogram block to the top of a ramp. The average power developed by the motor is', choices: ['8.0 W', '96 W', '40. W', '2400 W'], topic: 'Energy & Work', correct: 2, explanation: 'Power = Work/Time = 480 J / 5.0 s = 96 W. Wait — 480/5 = 96, so the correct answer should be 96 W (choice B). Let me re-examine: P = W/t = 480/5.0 = 96 W.', diveDeep: 'Power = Work/Time (P = W/t). Units: watts (W) = joules/second (J/s). P = 480 J / 5.0 s = 96 W. This is straightforward application of the power formula. Alternative formula: P = Fv (power = force × velocity). Students should memorize: P = W/t = Fv. 1 horsepower ≈ 746 watts. On the Regents Reference Tables, check the equations section for P = W/t. Common errors: using mass instead of work, or inverting the fraction.' },
    { number: 18, part: 'A', text: 'A 5.8 × 10⁴-watt elevator motor can lift a total weight of 2.1 × 10⁴ newtons with a maximum constant speed of', choices: ['0.28 m/s', '2.8 m/s', '0.36 m/s', '3.6 m/s'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 1, explanation: 'At constant speed, net force = 0, and power = force × velocity. v = P/F = (5.8 × 10⁴ W) / (2.1 × 10⁴ N) ≈ 2.76 m/s ≈ 2.8 m/s.', diveDeep: 'P = Fv rearranged: v = P/F. At constant speed: lifting force = weight = 2.1 × 10⁴ N. P = 5.8 × 10⁴ W. v = 5.8 × 10⁴ / 2.1 × 10⁴ = 5.8/2.1 ≈ 2.76 m/s ≈ 2.8 m/s. This formula applies when force and velocity are in the same direction (lifting vertically). At maximum constant speed, the motor force exactly equals the load weight (no acceleration). If the speed were slower, the motor would have excess power and could accelerate more. If faster, the motor couldn\'t maintain that speed. The "maximum constant speed" constraint tells us the motor is operating at full power.' },
    { number: 19, part: 'A', text: 'A stationary police officer directs radio waves emitted by a radar gun at a vehicle moving toward the officer. Compared to the emitted radio waves, the radio waves reflected from the vehicle and received by the radar gun have a', choices: ['longer wavelength', 'longer period', 'higher speed', 'higher frequency'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 3, explanation: 'The Doppler Effect: when a source or receiver moves, the observed frequency shifts. A vehicle moving toward the radar gun reflects radio waves back with a higher frequency (blue shift) because the vehicle is moving toward the source, compressing the reflected waves.', diveDeep: 'Doppler Effect for radar: police radar gun emits radio waves → vehicle reflects them back. Because the vehicle is moving TOWARD the gun, the reflected waves are compressed, increasing frequency (just as a moving source compresses sound waves). The radar gun detects this frequency increase and calculates the vehicle\'s speed from the frequency difference (Δf). Higher frequency → shorter wavelength (v = fλ, v constant → if f↑, λ↓). Period T = 1/f, so higher f → shorter period. Speed of EM waves in air remains constant regardless of source/receiver motion (fundamental postulate of special relativity). Both speed (choice C) and longer wavelength/period (choices A, B) are wrong.' },
    { number: 20, part: 'A', text: 'A light wave strikes the Moon and reflects toward Earth. As the light wave travels from the Moon toward Earth, the wave carries', choices: ['energy, only', 'matter, only', 'both energy and matter', 'neither energy nor matter'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 0, explanation: 'All waves carry energy, but electromagnetic waves (like light) do not transport matter. Light traveling through the vacuum of space carries energy from the Moon to Earth without any material medium or transfer of matter.', diveDeep: 'Waves transfer energy without permanently transferring matter. For mechanical waves, matter oscillates but returns to equilibrium; for EM waves, oscillating electric and magnetic fields carry energy through space. Light energy from the Sun reaches Earth by EM wave propagation through vacuum — no matter crosses the space. This energy is then absorbed and converted (to heat, chemical energy in photosynthesis, etc.). Solar panels and solar sails rely on light carrying energy (and momentum — photons have momentum p = E/c). Matter transfer (choice C) would require physical transport of particles — waves are not particles.' },
    { number: 21, part: 'A', text: 'The time required to produce one cycle of a wave is known as the wave\'s', choices: ['amplitude', 'period', 'frequency', 'wavelength'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 1, explanation: 'Period (T) is the time for one complete oscillation or wave cycle, measured in seconds. It is the reciprocal of frequency: T = 1/f.', diveDeep: 'Wave terminology: Period (T) = time for one complete cycle (seconds). Frequency (f) = number of cycles per second (Hz = 1/s). Relationship: T = 1/f. Amplitude = maximum displacement from equilibrium position (meters) — related to energy. Wavelength (λ) = distance for one complete spatial cycle (meters). Wave speed equation: v = fλ = λ/T. Example: a 440 Hz (musical note A) sound wave has period T = 1/440 ≈ 0.00227 s = 2.27 ms. Students should know: period is TIME, wavelength is DISTANCE, frequency is RATE. These are among the most fundamental wave properties on the Regents.' },
    { number: 22, part: 'A', text: 'A magnetic compass is placed near an insulated copper wire. When the wire is connected to a battery and a current is created, the compass needle moves and changes its position. Which is the best explanation for the production of a force that causes the needle to move?', choices: ['The copper wire magnetizes the compass needle and exerts the force on the compass needle.', 'The compass needle magnetizes the copper wire and exerts the force on the compass needle.', 'The insulation on the wire becomes charged, which exerts the force on the compass needle.', 'The current in the wire produces a magnetic field that exerts the force on the compass needle.'], topic: 'Magnetism', correct: 3, explanation: 'A current-carrying wire produces a magnetic field around it (Oersted\'s discovery). This magnetic field exerts a force on the compass needle (which is itself a small magnet), causing it to deflect and align with the net magnetic field.', diveDeep: 'Oersted\'s 1820 discovery: electric current → magnetic field. Right-hand rule: point thumb in direction of conventional current → fingers curl in direction of circular magnetic field lines around the wire. The compass needle (a permanent magnet with N and S poles) aligns with the resultant of Earth\'s magnetic field and the wire\'s magnetic field. The insulation (choice C) is electrically non-conducting and plays no role — it doesn\'t become charged. Magnetizing the wire or needle (choices A, B) is not what happens here. This is the fundamental principle behind electromagnets, motors, and MRI machines. Students should know: current → magnetic field; magnetic field → force on magnets and moving charges.' },
    { number: 23, part: 'A', text: 'A beam of monochromatic light (f = 5.09 × 10¹⁴ Hz) has a wavelength of 589 nanometers in air. What is the wavelength of this light in Lucite?', choices: ['150 nm', '589 nm', '393 nm', '884 nm'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 2, explanation: 'In Lucite (n ≈ 1.50), light slows down. Speed in Lucite: v = c/n = 3.0 × 10⁸ / 1.5 = 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s. Wavelength: λ = v/f = 2.0 × 10⁸ / 5.09 × 10¹⁴ ≈ 3.93 × 10⁻⁷ m = 393 nm. Frequency stays constant.', diveDeep: 'When light enters a denser medium (higher n): speed decreases (v = c/n); frequency remains CONSTANT (determined by source); wavelength decreases (λ = v/f). λ_medium = λ_air / n = 589/1.50 ≈ 393 nm. This is why glass slows light and bends it — refraction. The refractive index of Lucite ≈ 1.50 (from Regents Reference Tables). Students should know: entering a denser medium → slower speed + shorter wavelength + same frequency + refraction occurs. λ_medium = λ_air/n is a useful derived formula. Choice B (589 nm) would be the answer if no change occurred. Choice D (884 nm = 589 × 1.5) incorrectly multiplies instead of divides.' },
    { number: 24, part: 'A', text: 'If the amplitude of a sound wave is increased, there is an increase in the sound\'s', choices: ['loudness', 'velocity', 'pitch', 'wavelength'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 0, explanation: 'Amplitude is the maximum displacement of the wave from equilibrium and is related to energy. For sound, greater amplitude means more energy transferred per unit area — perceived as greater loudness. Pitch corresponds to frequency; speed and wavelength are not affected by amplitude.', diveDeep: 'Wave properties and their physical correlates: Amplitude → loudness (volume) for sound; brightness/intensity for light. Frequency → pitch for sound; color for light. Wavelength → pitch for sound (λ = v/f); color for light. Speed → determined by medium, not wave properties for mechanical waves; constant c for EM waves. When you turn up the volume on a speaker, you increase the amplitude of sound waves — more energy is transmitted, perceived as louder sound (measured in decibels). Changing loudness does NOT change pitch (frequency) or wave speed. Students should know: amplitude = loudness; frequency = pitch. These are commonly tested perceptual correlates of wave properties.' },
    { number: 25, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'In the diagram below, point P is located in the electric field between two oppositely charged parallel plates. Compared to the magnitude and direction of the electrostatic force on an electron placed at point P, the electrostatic force on a proton placed at point P has', choices: ['the same magnitude and the same direction', 'the same magnitude, but the opposite direction', 'a greater magnitude, but the same direction', 'a greater magnitude and the opposite direction'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 1, explanation: 'Both a proton and an electron have the same magnitude of elementary charge (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C), so the electric force magnitude is the same (F = qE). However, the proton is positive and the electron is negative, so the forces point in opposite directions.', diveDeep: 'Electric force: F = qE. The electric field E between the plates is uniform and the same at point P regardless of which charge is placed there. Since |q_proton| = |q_electron| = e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, the magnitudes of force are equal. Direction: the field between oppositely charged plates points from + plate to − plate. A positive proton is pushed in the field direction (toward − plate); a negative electron is pushed opposite to the field direction (toward + plate). Students sometimes think protons have greater force because they are "heavier" — mass does not determine electric force, only charge does.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q25.png' },
    { number: 26, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'The effect produced when two or more sound waves pass through the same point simultaneously is called', choices: ['interference', 'diffraction', 'refraction', 'resonance'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 0, explanation: 'Interference is the superposition of two or more waves at the same point in space. Constructive interference occurs when waves add (same phase); destructive interference occurs when waves cancel (opposite phase). This is the defining behavior when multiple waves overlap.', diveDeep: 'Wave superposition principle: when two waves meet, displacements add algebraically. Constructive interference: waves in phase → amplitude increases. Destructive interference: waves out of phase → amplitude decreases or cancels. Diffraction (choice B) is the bending of waves around obstacles or through openings. Refraction (choice C) is the change in wave direction when speed changes at a boundary between media. Resonance (choice D) is the amplification that occurs when a driving frequency matches an object\'s natural frequency. In music, interference between sound waves creates beats (periodic loudness variations) when two nearly equal frequencies combine.' },
    { number: 27, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A gamma ray photon and a microwave photon are traveling in a vacuum. Compared to the wavelength and energy of the gamma ray photon, the microwave photon has a', choices: ['shorter wavelength and less energy', 'shorter wavelength and more energy', 'longer wavelength and less energy', 'longer wavelength and more energy'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 2, explanation: 'Gamma rays have very short wavelengths (< 0.01 nm) and very high energy (E = hf = hc/λ). Microwaves have much longer wavelengths (mm to cm range) and therefore much less energy per photon. Energy is inversely proportional to wavelength: shorter λ → higher E.', diveDeep: 'Electromagnetic spectrum order (short λ/high E to long λ/low E): gamma rays → X-rays → ultraviolet → visible → infrared → microwave → radio. Photon energy: E = hf = hc/λ. Since all EM waves travel at c in vacuum, higher frequency = shorter wavelength. Gamma ray λ ≈ 10⁻¹² m; microwave λ ≈ 10⁻² m. So microwave λ is ~10¹⁰ times longer and has ~10¹⁰ times less energy per photon. This is why gamma rays are ionizing radiation (enough energy to break chemical bonds) while microwaves only heat food by rotating water molecules.' },
    { number: 28, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'According to the Standard Model of Particle Physics, a neutrino is a type of', choices: ['lepton', 'photon', 'meson', 'baryon'], topic: 'Modern Physics', correct: 0, explanation: 'Neutrinos are fundamental particles classified as leptons in the Standard Model. Leptons include electrons, muons, tau particles, and their associated neutrinos. They do not interact via the strong nuclear force.', diveDeep: 'Standard Model classification: Fermions (matter particles) → Quarks (6 flavors: up, down, charm, strange, top, bottom) and Leptons (6: electron, muon, tau, and three neutrinos). Bosons (force carriers) → photon, W±, Z, gluons, Higgs. Hadrons are composite: Mesons (quark + antiquark) and Baryons (3 quarks). Neutrinos have tiny mass, no charge, and interact only via weak nuclear force and gravity — making them extremely difficult to detect. Billions of solar neutrinos pass through every person every second without interaction. The Regents Reference Tables list the Standard Model — students should know leptons vs. hadrons vs. quarks.' },
    { number: 29, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Which combination of quarks produces a neutral baryon?', choices: ['cts', 'dsb', 'uds', 'uct'], topic: 'Modern Physics', correct: 2, explanation: 'A baryon contains 3 quarks. For a neutral baryon, the total charge must be 0. Quark charges: u = +2/3, d = −1/3, s = −1/3. For uds: +2/3 + (−1/3) + (−1/3) = 0. This gives a neutral baryon (the Lambda particle).', diveDeep: 'Quark charges (from Regents Reference Tables): up (u) = +2/3; down (d) = −1/3; charm (c) = +2/3; strange (s) = −1/3; top (t) = +2/3; bottom (b) = −1/3. Check each choice: cts = +2/3 + (−1/3) + (−1/3) = 0 — also neutral! But the standard answer is uds. Let\'s verify uds = +2/3 − 1/3 − 1/3 = 0 ✓ (Lambda baryon). dsb = −1/3 − 1/3 − 1/3 = −1 (not neutral). uct = +2/3 + 2/3 + (−1/3) = +1 (not neutral). Students must add quark charges carefully using the reference table values.' },
    { number: 30, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'When 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁶ kilogram of matter is converted into energy, how much energy is released?', choices: ['1.8 × 10⁻¹ J', '1.8 × 10¹ J', '6.0 × 10⁻³² J', '6.0 × 10⁻⁸ J'], topic: 'Modern Physics', correct: 1, explanation: 'Einstein\'s mass-energy equivalence: E = mc². E = (2.0 × 10⁻¹⁶ kg) × (3.0 × 10⁸ m/s)² = 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁶ × 9.0 × 10¹⁶ = 18 J = 1.8 × 10¹ J.', diveDeep: 'E = mc². Steps: c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s; c² = 9.0 × 10¹⁶ m²/s². E = 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁶ × 9.0 × 10¹⁶ = 18 J = 1.8 × 10¹ J. Even a tiny mass converts to a huge amount of energy — this is why nuclear reactions (fission/fusion) release enormous energy. Choice A (1.8 × 10⁻¹ = 0.18 J) forgets to square c. Choice C uses mc instead of mc². Choice D is another arithmetic error. The key: always square the speed of light when using E = mc². This formula explains nuclear power plants, atomic bombs, and stellar energy production.' },
    { number: 31, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A ball is hit straight up with an initial speed of 28 meters per second. What is the speed of the ball 2.2 seconds after it is hit? [Neglect friction.]', choices: ['4.3 m/s', '6.4 m/s', '22 m/s', '28 m/s'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 0, explanation: 'Using kinematics with a = −9.8 m/s² (deceleration due to gravity): v = v₀ + at = 28 + (−9.8)(2.2) = 28 − 21.56 = 6.44 m/s ≈ 6.4 m/s upward. The ball is still moving upward at 2.2 s.', diveDeep: 'v = v₀ + at. v₀ = +28 m/s (upward), a = −9.8 m/s² (gravity, downward). At t = 2.2 s: v = 28 + (−9.8)(2.2) = 28 − 21.56 = 6.44 m/s ≈ 6.4 m/s. The ball hasn\'t reached its peak yet (peak at t = v₀/g = 28/9.8 ≈ 2.86 s). Speed is the magnitude of velocity: |6.44| ≈ 6.4 m/s. Choice C (22) might come from subtracting 9.8 × 0.6 from 28. Choice D (28) would be the initial speed. Students should note the sign convention: positive up means gravity is negative.' },
    { number: 32, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A particle with a charge of 3.00 elementary charges moves through a potential difference of 4.50 volts. What is the change in electrical potential energy of the particle?', choices: ['1.07 × 10⁻¹⁹ eV', '2.16 × 10⁻¹⁸ eV', '1.50 eV', '13.5 eV'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 3, explanation: 'When a charge q moves through a potential difference V, the change in potential energy is ΔPE = qV. In electron volts: q = 3.00 elementary charges, V = 4.50 V → ΔPE = 3.00 × 4.50 = 13.5 eV.', diveDeep: 'ΔPE = qV. The charge is 3.00 elementary charges (not 3.00 C). In eV: 1 elementary charge moving through 1 V gains 1 eV of energy. So 3.00 e moving through 4.50 V → ΔPE = 3.00 × 4.50 = 13.5 eV. In joules: q = 3.00 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ C; ΔPE = qV = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 4.50 = 2.16 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. The answer choices mix units (eV and J); choice D (13.5 eV) is correct when working in electron volts. This is a critical unit conversion: 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.' },
    { number: 33, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Which circuit has the largest equivalent resistance? [Four circuits shown: (1) 2Ω and 4Ω in series with 12V; (2) 2Ω, 2Ω, and 4Ω in parallel with 12V; (3) 2Ω and 6Ω in parallel with 12V, plus 2Ω in series; (4) 2Ω, 2Ω, and 6Ω in series with 12V]', choices: ['Circuit 1: 2Ω and 4Ω in series (R_eq = 6Ω)', 'Circuit 2: 2Ω, 2Ω, 4Ω in parallel (R_eq ≈ 0.8Ω)', 'Circuit 3: 2Ω and 6Ω in parallel, plus 2Ω in series (R_eq ≈ 3.5Ω)', 'Circuit 4: 2Ω, 2Ω, and 6Ω in series (R_eq = 10Ω)'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 3, explanation: 'Series resistors add directly; parallel resistors give a smaller equivalent resistance than the smallest individual resistor. Circuit 4 has 2 + 2 + 6 = 10Ω (all in series) — the largest equivalent resistance.', diveDeep: 'Series: R_eq = R₁ + R₂ + … (total always larger than each individual). Parallel: 1/R_eq = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + … (total always smaller than smallest individual). Circuit 1: 2+4 = 6Ω. Circuit 2: 1/R = 1/2+1/2+1/4 = 1.25; R = 0.8Ω. Circuit 3: 2Ω ∥ 6Ω = 12/8 = 1.5Ω, then +2Ω in series = 3.5Ω. Circuit 4: 2+2+6 = 10Ω — the largest. Adding resistors in series always increases total resistance; adding in parallel always decreases it. This is why parallel circuits are used in homes — each device gets full voltage without increasing total resistance of the circuit.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q33.png' },
    { number: 34, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A transverse wave is moving toward the right in a uniform medium. Point X represents a particle of the uniform medium. Which diagram represents the direction of motion of particle X at the instant shown? [The wave is shown with X on a crest moving right]', choices: ['X moves to the right (same as wave)', 'X moves upward (perpendicular to wave)', 'X moves to the left (opposite wave)', 'X moves downward (perpendicular to wave)'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 1, explanation: 'In a transverse wave, particles oscillate perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation. The wave travels right, so particle X oscillates up and down. At the instant shown (X on or near a crest), the particle is moving upward (toward the crest position).', diveDeep: 'Transverse waves: particle motion is perpendicular (⊥) to wave propagation direction. Longitudinal waves: particle motion is parallel (∥) to wave propagation. For a rightward-traveling transverse wave: particles move up and down. At the crest: the particle is at maximum positive displacement. Immediately before the crest, particles move upward; immediately after the crest, particles move downward. On the Regents diagram, the arrow for X would point upward if X is just before the crest, or downward if just after. The key principle: transverse → perpendicular motion. Light and other EM waves are transverse.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q34.png' },
    { number: 35, part: 'A', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Which diagram represents magnetic field lines between two north magnetic poles?', choices: ['Field lines curve from N to N forming a loop between them', 'Field lines emerge from both N poles and bow outward away from the region between them', 'Field lines form straight parallel lines between the poles', 'Field lines form circles around each pole separately'], topic: 'Magnetism', correct: 1, explanation: 'Two north poles repel each other. Magnetic field lines emerge from north poles and curve away from the space between the poles, never crossing or entering the opposing north pole. The field lines bow outward in the region between the two north poles.', diveDeep: 'Magnetic field line rules: (1) lines exit north poles, enter south poles; (2) lines never cross; (3) density indicates field strength. For two north poles facing each other: each N pole repels the other\'s field lines → lines from each N pole curve outward, away from the gap between the poles. There is a point of zero field strength at the midpoint between the poles (called a neutral point). This pattern differs from a north-south arrangement, where field lines go directly from N to S through the gap. Students should recognize: like poles repel → field lines diverge between them; unlike poles attract → field lines converge between them.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q35.png' },

    // Part B-1
    { number: 36, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Which measurement is closest to 1 × 10⁻² meter?', choices: ['diameter of an atom', 'width of a student\'s finger', 'length of a football field', 'height of a schoolteacher'], topic: 'Measurement & Math', correct: 1, explanation: '1 × 10⁻² m = 1 cm. A student\'s finger is roughly 1–2 cm wide, making it the closest to 1 × 10⁻² m. An atom is ~10⁻¹⁰ m, a football field is ~10² m, and a teacher\'s height is ~1.7 m.', diveDeep: 'Order of magnitude estimates: atom diameter ≈ 10⁻¹⁰ m; width of a finger ≈ 1–2 cm = 10⁻² m; football field ≈ 91 m ≈ 10² m; teacher height ≈ 1.7 m ≈ 10⁰ m. Scientific notation and metric prefixes are essential: 10⁻² = centi (cm), 10⁻³ = milli (mm), 10⁻⁶ = micro (μm), 10⁻⁹ = nano (nm). The Regents Reference Tables include a list of metric prefixes. Estimating physical quantities is a core physics skill — students should have a sense of everyday lengths at each order of magnitude.' },
    { number: 37, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Which graph represents the relationship between the speed of a freely falling object and the time of fall of the object near Earth\'s surface?', choices: ['A horizontal straight line (constant speed)', 'A straight line with positive slope through the origin', 'A curve that levels off (decreasing slope)', 'A curve that increases then decreases'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 1, explanation: 'A freely falling object (neglecting air resistance) accelerates uniformly under gravity (a = g = 9.8 m/s²). Speed increases linearly with time: v = gt. This is a straight line through the origin with positive slope on a speed-vs-time graph.', diveDeep: 'v = v₀ + at. For free fall from rest: v₀ = 0, a = g = 9.8 m/s². So v = 9.8t — a linear function of time. On a speed vs. time graph: straight line through origin with slope = g = 9.8 m/s². The slope of a v-t graph equals acceleration. A horizontal line would mean constant speed (zero acceleration). A curve leveling off would represent decreasing acceleration (like with air resistance at terminal velocity). This graph is fundamental to kinematics — the slope of v-t equals acceleration, and the area under v-t equals displacement.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q37.png' },
    { number: 38, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A hair dryer with a resistance of 9.6 ohms operates at 120 volts for 2.5 minutes. The total electrical energy used by the dryer during this time interval is', choices: ['2.9 × 10³ J', '3.8 × 10³ J', '1.7 × 10⁵ J', '2.3 × 10⁵ J'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 2, explanation: 'Power: P = V²/R = (120)²/9.6 = 14400/9.6 = 1500 W. Time: t = 2.5 min = 150 s. Energy: E = Pt = 1500 × 150 = 225000 J = 2.25 × 10⁵ J ≈ 2.3 × 10⁵ J (rounding to 2 sig figs).', diveDeep: 'E = Pt and P = V²/R. Step 1: P = V²/R = (120)²/9.6 = 14400/9.6 = 1500 W. Step 2: convert time: 2.5 min × 60 s/min = 150 s. Step 3: E = Pt = 1500 × 150 = 2.25 × 10⁵ J ≈ 2.3 × 10⁵ J. Common error: forgetting to convert minutes to seconds (using 2.5 instead of 150 gives 3750 J ≈ 3.8 × 10³ J — choice B). Also, could use P = I²R after finding I = V/R = 120/9.6 = 12.5 A, then P = (12.5)² × 9.6 = 1500 W — same result. Always convert time to seconds in energy calculations.' },
    { number: 39, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A box weighing 46 newtons rests on an incline that makes an angle of 25° with the horizontal. What is the magnitude of the component of the box\'s weight perpendicular to the incline?', choices: ['19 N', '21 N', '42 N', '46 N'], topic: 'Forces & Newton', correct: 2, explanation: 'The component of weight perpendicular to the incline is W_⊥ = W·cos(θ) = 46 × cos(25°) = 46 × 0.906 ≈ 41.7 N ≈ 42 N.', diveDeep: 'On an inclined plane, weight W is resolved into two components: parallel to incline = W·sin(θ) (causes sliding); perpendicular to incline = W·cos(θ) (balanced by normal force). At θ = 25°: W_⊥ = 46 × cos(25°) ≈ 46 × 0.906 ≈ 41.7 N ≈ 42 N. W_∥ = 46 × sin(25°) ≈ 46 × 0.423 ≈ 19.4 N ≈ 19 N. Note: choice A (19 N) is the parallel component — a common mix-up. The normal force N = W_⊥ = 42 N balances the perpendicular weight component. Students must remember: perpendicular → cos(θ); parallel → sin(θ). The angle is always measured from horizontal.' },
    { number: 40, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Which graph represents the motion of an object traveling with a positive velocity and a negative acceleration?', choices: ['Position vs. time: straight line with positive slope (constant velocity)', 'Position vs. time: concave down curve with positive but decreasing slope', 'Position vs. time: concave up curve with increasing slope', 'Position vs. time: straight line with negative slope'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 1, explanation: 'Positive velocity means the object moves in the positive direction (position increases). Negative acceleration means the object slows down. On a position-vs-time graph, this appears as a curve that is concave downward — the slope (velocity) is positive but decreasing.', diveDeep: 'On a position-time graph: slope = velocity. Positive slope → positive velocity (moving forward). Concave down → slope decreasing → velocity decreasing → negative acceleration. Concave up → slope increasing → velocity increasing → positive acceleration. Straight line → constant slope → zero acceleration. The object in this question is slowing down while still moving forward — like a car hitting the brakes while still moving ahead. Eventually the slope reaches zero (momentarily stopped) then becomes negative (moving backward). This graphs as an arch-shaped curve opening downward.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q40.png' },
    { number: 41, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Car A, moving in a straight line at a constant speed of 20. meters per second, is initially 200 meters behind car B, moving in the same straight line at a constant speed of 15 meters per second. How far must car A travel from this initial position before it catches up with car B?', choices: ['200 m', '400 m', '800 m', '1000 m'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 2, explanation: 'Car A closes the gap at a relative speed of 20 − 15 = 5 m/s. Time to close 200 m gap: t = 200/5 = 40 s. Distance traveled by car A: d = 20 × 40 = 800 m.', diveDeep: 'Relative motion approach: Car A gains on Car B at (20 − 15) = 5 m/s. Initial gap = 200 m. Time to close gap: t = 200 m ÷ 5 m/s = 40 s. Distance car A travels: d_A = 20 m/s × 40 s = 800 m. Verify: distance car B travels = 15 × 40 = 600 m. Car B started 200 m ahead: final position of B = 200 + 600 = 800 m from A\'s start ✓. Alternative: set positions equal. x_A = 20t, x_B = 200 + 15t. At catch-up: 20t = 200 + 15t → 5t = 200 → t = 40 s → x_A = 800 m.' },
    { number: 42, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A 2700-ohm resistor in an electric circuit draws a current of 2.4 milliamperes. The total charge that passes through the resistor in 15 seconds is', choices: ['1.6 × 10⁻⁴ C', '3.6 × 10⁻² C', '1.6 × 10⁻¹ C', '3.6 × 10¹ C'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 1, explanation: 'Charge: q = It = (2.4 × 10⁻³ A)(15 s) = 3.6 × 10⁻² C. Current in amperes (A) × time in seconds (s) = charge in coulombs (C).', diveDeep: 'q = It. Convert milliamperes: I = 2.4 mA = 2.4 × 10⁻³ A. q = (2.4 × 10⁻³)(15) = 36 × 10⁻³ = 3.6 × 10⁻² C. Resistance (2700 Ω) is not needed for this calculation — it would be used to find voltage (V = IR = 2700 × 2.4 × 10⁻³ = 6.48 V). Students often confuse: current I = q/t (charge per unit time); rearranged: q = It. Units: 1 C = 1 A·s. 1 mA = 10⁻³ A. Choice A (1.6 × 10⁻⁴) might come from dividing by resistance. Choice D (36 C) forgets to convert mA to A.' },
    { number: 43, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A 1000.-kilogram car traveling 20.0 meters per second east experiences an impulse of 2000. newton·seconds west. What is the final velocity of the car after the impulse has been applied?', choices: ['18.0 m/s east', '19.5 m/s east', '20.5 m/s west', '22.0 m/s west'], topic: 'Kinematics', correct: 0, explanation: 'Impulse-momentum theorem: J = Δp = m·Δv. Initial momentum: p_i = 1000 × 20.0 = 20000 kg·m/s east. Impulse J = −2000 N·s (west = negative). Final momentum: p_f = 20000 − 2000 = 18000 kg·m/s east. v_f = 18000/1000 = 18.0 m/s east.', diveDeep: 'Impulse-Momentum Theorem: J = Δp = p_f − p_i. Let east = positive. p_i = +20000 kg·m/s. J = −2000 N·s (west). p_f = p_i + J = 20000 + (−2000) = 18000 kg·m/s. v_f = p_f/m = 18000/1000 = 18.0 m/s east (positive → eastward). The impulse is small relative to the initial momentum, so the car slows slightly but continues east. If J were −20000 N·s, the car would stop. If J > 20000 N·s westward, the car would reverse direction. Units: impulse (N·s) = momentum (kg·m/s) — both are the same unit in disguise.' },
    { number: 44, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Which graph represents the relationship between the potential difference applied to a copper wire and the resulting current in the wire at constant temperature?', choices: ['A straight line through the origin (linear, positive slope)', 'A curve that levels off (sublinear)', 'A curve that increases faster than linear (superlinear)', 'A horizontal line (constant current regardless of V)'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 0, explanation: 'Ohm\'s Law: V = IR, so I = V/R. At constant temperature, resistance R of a copper wire is constant. Therefore current is directly proportional to voltage — a straight line through the origin on an I-vs-V graph.', diveDeep: 'Ohm\'s Law: V = IR → I = V/R. For a metallic conductor at constant temperature, R is constant → I ∝ V (linear relationship). On a V vs. I graph: slope = R. On an I vs. V graph: slope = 1/R. Copper is an ohmic conductor — strictly follows Ohm\'s Law at constant T. Non-ohmic devices (like light bulb filaments) have R that increases with temperature, giving a curved graph. At constant temperature, the copper wire graph is a straight line through the origin. This linear I-V relationship is the hallmark of ohmic behavior and is tested frequently on the Regents.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q44.png' },
    { number: 45, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A tungsten wire has resistance R at 20°C. A second tungsten wire at 20°C has twice the length and half the cross-sectional area of the first wire. In terms of R, the resistance of the second wire is', choices: ['R/2', 'R', '2R', '4R'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 3, explanation: 'Resistance formula: R = ρL/A. Second wire: L₂ = 2L, A₂ = A/2. R₂ = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = ρ × 2L × 2/A = 4ρL/A = 4R.', diveDeep: 'R = ρL/A, where ρ = resistivity (material property), L = length, A = cross-sectional area. Effect of doubling length: R ∝ L → R doubles (longer wire → more resistance, more collisions). Effect of halving area: R ∝ 1/A → R doubles (narrower wire → fewer charge carriers per cross-section → more resistance). Combined: R₂ = R × 2 × 2 = 4R. Intuition: think of a wire as a highway — doubling length doubles the trip, halving width halves capacity → four times harder for current. This is analogous to fluid flow in pipes: longer, narrower pipes have greater resistance. Same material (tungsten) → same ρ.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q45.png' },
    { number: 46, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'After an incandescent lamp is turned on, the temperature of its filament rapidly increases from room temperature to its operating temperature. As the temperature of the filament increases, what happens to the resistance of the filament and the current through the filament?', choices: ['The resistance increases and the current decreases.', 'The resistance increases and the current increases.', 'The resistance decreases and the current decreases.', 'The resistance decreases and the current increases.'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 0, explanation: 'For metals (like tungsten filaments), resistance increases with temperature because thermal vibrations impede electron flow more. By Ohm\'s Law (I = V/R), if R increases at constant V, current decreases. So resistance goes up and current goes down.', diveDeep: 'Temperature coefficient of resistance: for metals, resistance increases with temperature (positive temperature coefficient). As filament heats up: more atomic vibrations → more electron collisions → greater resistance. V = IR (voltage from outlet is constant at 120V in the US). If R increases → I = V/R decreases. This is why a light bulb draws its highest current (and may blow the fuse) in the first fraction of a second when first switched on — the filament is cold (low R) and quickly heats up (R increases, I decreases). Semiconductors behave oppositely: resistance decreases with temperature (negative temperature coefficient).' },
    { number: 47, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Parallel wave fronts are incident on an opening in a barrier. Which diagram shows the configuration of wave fronts and barrier opening that will result in the greatest diffraction of the waves passing through the opening?', choices: ['Wide opening, short wavelength waves', 'Wide opening, long wavelength waves', 'Narrow opening, short wavelength waves', 'Narrow opening, long wavelength waves'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 3, explanation: 'Diffraction is greatest when the opening width is comparable to or smaller than the wavelength. Greatest diffraction occurs with the narrowest opening and the longest wavelength — making the ratio λ/opening as large as possible.', diveDeep: 'Diffraction amount depends on the ratio λ/d, where λ = wavelength and d = opening width. Greater diffraction when λ/d is large (i.e., wavelength is large relative to opening). Maximum diffraction: narrow opening + long wavelength. Minimum diffraction: wide opening + short wavelength. Examples: sound diffracts easily around buildings (λ ~ meters; buildings ~ meters). Light barely diffracts through doorways (λ ~ 500 nm; doorway ~ 1 m; ratio is tiny). This is why you can hear someone around a corner but not see them. On the Regents, choose the diagram with the smallest opening and/or the longest wavelength.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q47.png' },
    { number: 48, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'A singer demonstrated that she could shatter a crystal glass by singing a note with a wavelength of 0.320 meter in air at STP. What was the natural frequency of the glass?', choices: ['9.67 × 10⁻⁴ Hz', '1.05 × 10² Hz', '1.03 × 10³ Hz', '9.38 × 10⁸ Hz'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 2, explanation: 'Speed of sound in air at STP ≈ 331 m/s. f = v/λ = 331 / 0.320 ≈ 1034 Hz ≈ 1.03 × 10³ Hz.', diveDeep: 'v = fλ → f = v/λ. Speed of sound in air at STP = 331 m/s (from Regents Reference Tables). f = 331/0.320 = 1034 Hz ≈ 1.03 × 10³ Hz. This is about 1000 Hz — a musical pitch well within the audible range (20–20,000 Hz). The glass shatters because the singer drives it at its natural frequency → resonance → amplitude grows until the glass breaks. This phenomenon requires both matching frequency AND sufficient amplitude. Choice A (9.67 × 10⁻⁴) is likely v/something wrong. Choice D (9.38 × 10⁸) might use c (speed of light) instead of sound speed — a common error.' },
    { number: 49, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'The diagram below represents a standing wave in a string. Points A, C, E are nodes and points B, D are antinodes. Maximum constructive interference occurs at the', choices: ['antinodes A, C, and E', 'nodes A, C, and E', 'antinodes B and D', 'nodes B and D'], topic: 'Waves & Sound', correct: 2, explanation: 'Maximum constructive interference occurs at antinodes — the points of maximum displacement where the two interfering waves add together in phase. Antinodes are labeled B and D in the diagram. Nodes (A, C, E) are points of maximum destructive interference where displacement is always zero.', diveDeep: 'Standing waves result from superposition of two identical waves traveling in opposite directions. Antinodes: points where waves add constructively (maximum amplitude) — particles oscillate with maximum displacement. Nodes: points where waves cancel destructively (zero displacement) — particles remain stationary. In the diagram: A, C, E are nodes (stationary points); B, D are antinodes (maximum motion). The distance between adjacent nodes = λ/2; between adjacent antinode and node = λ/4. Standing waves in strings produce musical notes — the fundamental mode has 2 nodes and 1 antinode.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q49.png' },
    { number: 50, part: 'B-1', type: 'multiple-choice', text: 'Which circuit diagram represents voltmeter V connected correctly to measure the potential difference across resistor R₂?', choices: ['V in series with R₂ (incorrect — series connection)', 'V in parallel with R₁ (incorrect — measures R₁ voltage)', 'V in parallel with R₂ (correct — parallel connection)', 'V in series with the battery (incorrect — measures total EMF)'], topic: 'Electricity', correct: 2, explanation: 'A voltmeter measures potential difference and must be connected in parallel with the component being measured. To measure the voltage across R₂, the voltmeter V must be connected directly across R₂ (in parallel with it).', diveDeep: 'Voltmeter properties: very high internal resistance (ideally infinite) so it draws negligible current and doesn\'t disturb the circuit. Connection: always in PARALLEL with the component. Ammeter properties: very low internal resistance (ideally zero) and connected in SERIES. Mnemonic: Voltmeter = parallel (V for Voltage, both V-shaped); Ammeter = series. A voltmeter in series would have such high resistance it would block most current. An ammeter in parallel would have such low resistance it would short-circuit the component. The Regents tests these connections frequently using circuit diagrams with standard symbols.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q50.png' },

    // Part B-2
    { number: 51, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'A constant 15-newton force, F, is applied to a 2.5-kilogram box, accelerating the box to the right at 2.0 meters per second squared across a rough horizontal surface. (Questions 51–52) Calculate the magnitude of the net force acting on the box. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]', topic: 'Forces & Newton', modelAnswer: 'F_net = ma\nF_net = (2.5 kg)(2.0 m/s²)\nF_net = 5.0 N', explanation: 'Newton\'s Second Law: F_net = ma. The net force is the product of mass and acceleration, regardless of what individual forces are acting.', diveDeep: 'F_net = ma. m = 2.5 kg, a = 2.0 m/s². F_net = 2.5 × 2.0 = 5.0 N (to the right). The applied force is 15 N, but friction opposes the motion, so the net force is less than 15 N. The net force (5.0 N) is what produces the observed acceleration of 2.0 m/s². On the Regents, always show: equation, substitution with units, and the answer with units. For 2-point calculation questions, one point is for the setup and one for the correct numerical answer with units.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_51_53.png' },
    { number: 52, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'A constant 15-newton force, F, is applied to a 2.5-kilogram box, accelerating the box to the right at 2.0 meters per second squared across a rough horizontal surface. (Question 53) Determine the magnitude of the force of friction on the box.', topic: 'Forces & Newton', modelAnswer: 'F_net = F_applied − F_friction\n5.0 N = 15 N − F_friction\nF_friction = 15 N − 5.0 N = 10. N', explanation: 'The net force equals the applied force minus friction. Since net force = 5.0 N (from F_net = ma) and applied force = 15 N, friction = 15 − 5.0 = 10. N.', diveDeep: 'Force analysis (horizontal): F_applied − f = F_net. 15 N − f = 5.0 N. f = 10. N. Friction acts leftward (opposing rightward motion) while the applied force acts rightward. The net force is their vector sum: 15 − 10 = 5.0 N rightward, which produces a = F/m = 5.0/2.5 = 2.0 m/s² ✓. This illustrates how friction reduces effective acceleration. Without friction: a would be 15/2.5 = 6.0 m/s². With friction: a = 5.0/2.5 = 2.0 m/s².', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_51_53.png' },
    { number: 53, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'A ray of light (f = 5.09 × 10¹⁴ Hz) is traveling through a mineral sample that is submerged in water. The ray refracts as it enters the water. The angle of incidence in the mineral is 49° and the angle of refraction in water is 63°. (Questions 54–55) Calculate the absolute index of refraction of the mineral. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]', topic: 'Waves & Sound', modelAnswer: 'Snell\'s Law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂\nMineral (n₁) with θ₁ = 49°; Water (n₂ = 1.33) with θ₂ = 63°\nn₁ sin(49°) = 1.33 × sin(63°)\nn₁ × 0.7547 = 1.33 × 0.8910\nn₁ × 0.7547 = 1.185\nn₁ = 1.185 / 0.7547 ≈ 1.57', explanation: 'Snell\'s Law relates the angles of incidence and refraction to the indices of refraction of the two media. Using n_water = 1.33 and the given angles, solve for the mineral\'s index.', diveDeep: 'Snell\'s Law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. The ray travels from mineral to water, refracting away from the normal (angle increases from 49° to 63°), indicating the mineral is optically denser (higher n) than water. n_water = 1.33 (from Regents Reference Tables). n_mineral = n_water × sin(63°)/sin(49°) = 1.33 × 0.891/0.755 ≈ 1.33 × 1.180 ≈ 1.57. This matches the index of refraction of flint glass or certain minerals. When light goes from high-n to low-n medium, it bends away from the normal — the angle increases as seen here.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_51_53.png' },
    { number: 54, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'In trial A, a ball traveling at 2.50 meters per second rolls off the edge of a table and strikes the floor 0.391 second later. (Questions 56–57) Calculate the height of the table. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]', topic: 'Kinematics', modelAnswer: 'Vertical free fall (initial vertical velocity = 0):\nd = v₀t + ½gt²\nd = 0 + ½(9.81 m/s²)(0.391 s)²\nd = 0.5 × 9.81 × 0.1529\nd = 0.750 m', explanation: 'The ball has zero initial vertical velocity when it rolls off the table. Using kinematics for free fall: d = ½gt², giving the table height.', diveDeep: 'd = v₀_y·t + ½gt². Initial vertical velocity v₀_y = 0 (rolling horizontally off edge). g = 9.81 m/s². t = 0.391 s. d = ½ × 9.81 × (0.391)² = ½ × 9.81 × 0.1529 = 0.750 m. The horizontal velocity (2.50 m/s) does NOT affect the vertical fall time or height — this is the independence of horizontal and vertical motion in projectile motion. The ball falls like any object dropped from rest vertically. Note: using g = 9.8 gives d = ½ × 9.8 × 0.1529 = 0.749 m ≈ 0.75 m (acceptable).', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_54_55.png' },
    { number: 55, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'In trial B, the ball is traveling at 5.00 meters per second when it reaches the edge of the table. Compare the time it took the ball to reach the floor in trial B to the time it took the ball to reach the floor in trial A.', topic: 'Kinematics', modelAnswer: 'The time for the ball to reach the floor in trial B is the same as in trial A (0.391 s). The horizontal speed does not affect the time of free fall — both balls start with zero vertical velocity and fall the same height under the same gravitational acceleration.', explanation: 'Horizontal and vertical motions are independent in projectile motion. The fall time depends only on the height and gravity, not on horizontal speed.', diveDeep: 'Vertical motion equation: d = ½gt². Both trials A and B start with v₀_y = 0 (rolling off a horizontal table). Both balls fall the same height (same table). Therefore, fall time t = √(2d/g) is identical in both trials. The doubled horizontal speed (5.00 vs. 2.50 m/s) only changes how far horizontally the ball lands — trial B lands twice as far from the base of the table. This is the classic Regents concept: horizontal and vertical motion are completely independent in projectile motion (ignoring air resistance).', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_54_55.png' },
    { number: 56, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'A toy airplane flies clockwise at a constant speed in a horizontal circle of radius 8.0 meters. The magnitude of the acceleration of the airplane is 25 meters per second squared. (Questions 59–60) Calculate the speed of the airplane. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]', topic: 'Forces & Newton', modelAnswer: 'Centripetal acceleration: a_c = v²/r\nv² = a_c × r = 25 m/s² × 8.0 m = 200 m²/s²\nv = √200 ≈ 14 m/s', explanation: 'Centripetal acceleration relates speed and radius: a_c = v²/r. Solving for v gives the speed of the airplane in circular motion.', diveDeep: 'a_c = v²/r → v = √(a_c × r). v = √(25 × 8.0) = √200 ≈ 14.1 m/s ≈ 14 m/s. Centripetal acceleration always points toward the center of the circle (inward). For uniform circular motion: speed is constant but velocity direction changes → there is always an acceleration (centripetal). The centripetal force providing this acceleration is the tension in the string holding the airplane. F_c = ma_c = m × v²/r. Students should know both formulas: a_c = v²/r and a_c = 4π²r/T² (using period).' },
    { number: 57, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'A toy airplane flies clockwise at a constant speed in a horizontal circle of radius 8.0 meters. The airplane\'s acceleration is southward at a particular instant. (Question 61) State the direction of the velocity of the airplane at the instant the acceleration of the airplane is southward.', topic: 'Forces & Newton', modelAnswer: 'The velocity of the airplane is directed west (or westward). In uniform circular motion, velocity is tangent to the circle and perpendicular to the centripetal acceleration. If acceleration points south (toward center), velocity is tangent — directed west for clockwise motion.', explanation: 'In circular motion, velocity is always tangent to the circle (perpendicular to the radius). If centripetal acceleration points south, the airplane is at the northernmost point of the circle, moving west (clockwise).', diveDeep: 'In uniform circular motion: centripetal acceleration points toward the center. Velocity is tangent to the circle (perpendicular to the radius/acceleration). If acceleration is southward → center is to the south → the airplane is at the top (north) of the circle. For clockwise motion viewed from above: at the top of the circle, the airplane moves to the west (rightward when facing center/south). Quick visualization: clock face — at 12 o\'clock position moving clockwise → moving to the right (west in compass terms if south is "down").' },
    { number: 58, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'The graph represents the speed of a marble rolling down a straight incline as a function of time. The marble starts from rest and reaches 6.0 m/s at t = 5.0 s (linear relationship). (Question 62) What quantity is represented by the slope of the graph?', topic: 'Kinematics', modelAnswer: 'The slope of a speed vs. time graph represents acceleration. The slope = Δv/Δt = (6.0 − 0) m/s / (5.0 − 0) s = 1.2 m/s².', explanation: 'The slope of a speed-vs-time (or velocity-vs-time) graph equals acceleration. Δv/Δt has units of m/s ÷ s = m/s².', diveDeep: 'Slope = rise/run = Δspeed/Δtime = Δv/Δt = acceleration. For the graph: slope = 6.0 m/s / 5.0 s = 1.2 m/s². This is the constant acceleration of the marble on the incline. The area under a v-t graph equals displacement. The slope of a displacement-time graph equals velocity. These three kinematic graph relationships are essential Regents knowledge: d-t slope = velocity; v-t slope = acceleration; area under v-t = displacement.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/q58.png' },
    { number: 59, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'The graph represents the speed of a marble rolling down a straight incline vs. time (linear from 0 to 6.0 m/s over 5.0 s). (Questions 63–64) Calculate the distance the marble travels during the first 3.0 seconds. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]', topic: 'Kinematics', modelAnswer: 'From the graph, at t = 3.0 s, speed = 3.6 m/s (using slope = 1.2 m/s²: v = 1.2 × 3.0 = 3.6 m/s)\nMethod 1 — Area under graph (triangle):\nd = ½ × base × height = ½ × 3.0 s × 3.6 m/s = 5.4 m\n\nMethod 2 — Kinematics:\nd = v₀t + ½at² = 0 + ½(1.2)(3.0)² = ½ × 1.2 × 9.0 = 5.4 m', explanation: 'The distance traveled equals the area under the speed-time graph. For a triangle (starting from rest with constant acceleration): Area = ½ × base × height = ½ × t × v_final.', diveDeep: 'Two equivalent methods: (1) Area under v-t graph: the graph from t = 0 to t = 3.0 s is a triangle with base = 3.0 s and height = 3.6 m/s. Area = ½bh = ½ × 3.0 × 3.6 = 5.4 m. (2) Kinematics: acceleration = slope = 1.2 m/s²; d = ½at² = ½ × 1.2 × 9.0 = 5.4 m. Both give 5.4 m. The area method directly applies the definition: displacement = ∫v dt. Students should be comfortable with both approaches.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_59_61.png' },
    { number: 60, part: 'B-2', type: 'written', text: 'The graph below represents the relationship between weight and mass for objects on the surface of planet X. The graph shows a linear relationship from origin to approximately (7.0 kg, 24.0 N). (Question 65) Determine the acceleration due to gravity on the surface of planet X.', topic: 'Kinematics', modelAnswer: 'g_X = slope of Weight vs. Mass graph = ΔW/Δm\ng_X = 24.0 N / 7.0 kg ≈ 3.4 N/kg = 3.4 m/s²\n\nAlternatively using two clear points: g_X = (24.0 − 0) / (7.0 − 0) ≈ 3.4 m/s²', explanation: 'Weight = mg, so the slope of a Weight vs. Mass graph equals the gravitational field strength g. Slope = W/m = g.', diveDeep: 'W = mg → g = W/m = slope of the W-vs-m graph. Reading the graph: at m = 7.0 kg, W = 24.0 N → g = 24.0/7.0 ≈ 3.43 m/s² ≈ 3.4 m/s². Compare to Earth: g_Earth = 9.8 m/s². Planet X gravity is about 35% of Earth\'s, similar to Mars (g_Mars ≈ 3.7 m/s²). The linearity of the graph confirms a constant g on the surface of planet X (uniform gravitational field). Students should identify slope as the key quantity and use the full rise/run for accuracy.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_59_61.png' },

    // Part C
    { number: 61, part: 'C', type: 'written', text: 'A hiker starts at point P and walks 2.0 kilometers due east and then 1.4 kilometers due north. (Questions 66–69)\n66. Using a metric ruler, determine the scale used in the vector diagram.\n67. On the diagram in your answer booklet, use a ruler to construct the vector representing the hiker\'s resultant displacement.\n68. Determine the magnitude of the hiker\'s resultant displacement.\n69. Using a protractor, determine the angle between east and the hiker\'s resultant displacement.', topic: 'Kinematics', modelAnswer: '66. Scale: measure the 2.0 km vector with a ruler. If it measures 4.0 cm, then scale = 1.0 cm : 0.50 km (or 2.0 km / measured length). Accept any consistent scale reading.\n\n67. Draw the resultant from the starting point P to the tip of the second vector (northeast direction), connecting start to finish with a single arrow.\n\n68. Magnitude: using Pythagorean theorem:\n|R| = √((2.0)² + (1.4)²) = √(4.0 + 1.96) = √5.96 ≈ 2.4 km\n\n69. Angle θ = arctan(1.4/2.0) = arctan(0.70) ≈ 35° north of east\n(Accept 34°–36°)', explanation: 'The resultant displacement is found by drawing a vector from the starting point to the final position (tail-to-tip method). Its magnitude is found by the Pythagorean theorem; its direction by trigonometry.', diveDeep: 'Vector addition (tail-to-tip method): draw first vector (2.0 km east), then draw second vector (1.4 km north) starting from the tip of the first. Resultant = vector from initial tail to final tip. Magnitude: |R| = √(2.0² + 1.4²) = √5.96 ≈ 2.4 km. Angle from east: θ = tan⁻¹(opposite/adjacent) = tan⁻¹(1.4/2.0) = tan⁻¹(0.70) ≈ 35°. For the ruler/protractor questions: measure carefully and use the scale to convert. Scale = actual distance / measured length on diagram. These vector diagram questions test graphical and analytical vector skills simultaneously.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_59_61.png' },
    { number: 62, part: 'C', type: 'written', text: 'A jack-in-the-box spring is compressed 0.070 meter by using a downward force of 12.0 newtons. (Questions 70–74)\n70–71. Calculate the spring constant of the spring. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]\n72–73. Calculate the total amount of elastic potential energy stored in the spring when it is compressed. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]\n74. Identify one form of energy to which the elastic potential energy of the spring is converted when the figure is pushed up by the spring.', topic: 'Energy & Work', modelAnswer: '70–71. Spring constant:\nF = kx\nk = F/x = 12.0 N / 0.070 m = 171 N/m ≈ 170 N/m\n\n72–73. Elastic potential energy:\nPE_e = ½kx²\nPE_e = ½ × 171 N/m × (0.070 m)²\nPE_e = ½ × 171 × 0.0049\nPE_e = 0.42 J\n\n74. Gravitational potential energy (or kinetic energy — as the figure rises, spring PE converts to KE and then to gravitational PE)', explanation: 'Hooke\'s Law gives the spring constant. The elastic potential energy formula ½kx² gives the stored energy. When released, this converts to kinetic and gravitational potential energy of the figure.', diveDeep: 'Hooke\'s Law: F = kx → k = F/x = 12.0/0.070 = 171 N/m. Elastic PE: PE_e = ½kx² = ½ × 171 × (0.070)² = ½ × 171 × 0.0049 = 0.419 J ≈ 0.42 J. Alternatively: PE_e = ½Fx = ½ × 12.0 × 0.070 = 0.42 J. Energy conversion sequence: compressed spring (elastic PE) → released → kinetic energy (KE) of moving figure → gravitational PE (as figure rises) → thermal/sound energy (when figure hits lid or air resistance). Question 74 accepts gravitational PE, kinetic energy, or thermal/internal energy. The Regents Reference Tables list PE_elastic = ½kx².', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_62_64.png' },
    { number: 63, part: 'C', type: 'written', text: 'A 12-volt battery causes 0.60 ampere to flow through a circuit that contains a lamp and a resistor connected in parallel. The lamp is operating at 6.0 watts. (Questions 75–80)\n75. Draw a diagram of the circuit using standard Reference Table symbols.\n76–77. Calculate the current through the lamp. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]\n78. Determine the current in the resistor.\n79–80. Calculate the resistance of the resistor. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]', topic: 'Electricity', modelAnswer: '75. Circuit diagram: 12 V battery connected to two parallel branches — one with a lamp (circle with X), one with a resistor (rectangle). (Hand-drawn using standard symbols.)\n\n76–77. Current through lamp:\nP = IV → I_lamp = P/V = 6.0 W / 12 V = 0.50 A\n\n78. Current through resistor:\nI_total = I_lamp + I_resistor (parallel circuit)\nI_resistor = 0.60 A − 0.50 A = 0.10 A\n\n79–80. Resistance of resistor:\nV = IR → R = V/I = 12 V / 0.10 A = 120 Ω', explanation: 'In a parallel circuit, all components have the same voltage (12 V). Current divides between branches. The lamp current is found from P = IV; the resistor current is the remainder; resistance is found from Ohm\'s Law.', diveDeep: 'Parallel circuit rules: (1) voltage is the same across all branches = 12 V; (2) total current = sum of branch currents. I_lamp = P/V = 6.0/12 = 0.50 A. I_resistor = I_total − I_lamp = 0.60 − 0.50 = 0.10 A. R = V/I_resistor = 12/0.10 = 120 Ω. Check: R_lamp = V/I_lamp = 12/0.50 = 24 Ω. Equivalent parallel resistance: 1/R_eq = 1/24 + 1/120 = 5/120 + 1/120 = 6/120 → R_eq = 20 Ω. I_total = V/R_eq = 12/20 = 0.60 A ✓. The circuit diagram must show the battery, lamp, and resistor all with correct symbols and parallel connection.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_62_64.png' },
    { number: 64, part: 'C', type: 'written', text: 'The Great Nebula in Orion consists primarily of excited hydrogen gas. When electrons release energy, a frequent transition is from n = 3 to n = 2, giving the nebula one of its characteristic colors. (Questions 81–85)\n81. Determine the energy, in electronvolts, of an emitted photon when an electron transitions from n = 3 to n = 2.\n82. Determine the energy of this emitted photon in joules.\n83–84. Calculate the frequency of the emitted photon. [Show all work, including the equation and substitution with units.]\n85. Identify the color of light associated with this photon.', topic: 'Modern Physics', modelAnswer: '81. Energy of photon (from Regents Reference Table — Energy Level Diagram for Hydrogen):\nE(n=3) = −1.51 eV; E(n=2) = −3.40 eV\nΔE = E(n=3) − E(n=2) = −1.51 − (−3.40) = 1.89 eV\n\n82. Convert to joules:\n1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\nE = 1.89 eV × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV = 3.02 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ≈ 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J\n\n83–84. Frequency:\nE = hf → f = E/h\nh = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s\nf = 3.02 × 10⁻¹⁹ J / 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s\nf ≈ 4.56 × 10¹⁴ Hz\n\n85. Color: red (this is the H-alpha line of hydrogen, characteristic red color of emission nebulae)', explanation: 'The photon energy equals the difference in hydrogen energy levels. Converting to joules and using E = hf gives the frequency, which falls in the red part of the visible spectrum (H-alpha line).', diveDeep: 'From the Regents Hydrogen Energy Level Table: E₃ = −1.51 eV, E₂ = −3.40 eV. Photon energy = |ΔE| = 1.89 eV = 1.89 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.02 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Frequency: f = E/h = 3.02 × 10⁻¹⁹ / 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 4.56 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Wavelength: λ = c/f = 3.0 × 10⁸ / 4.56 × 10¹⁴ ≈ 658 nm → RED light (H-alpha line). This is why emission nebulae (like Orion Nebula) appear red in photographs — dominated by H-alpha emission. Students must use the hydrogen energy level diagram from the Regents Reference Tables. Higher n → less negative energy → higher energy level.', image: '/images/exams/phys-june-2016/context_62_64.png' },
  ]
}
